::: {.doc-subject}

Get edges as a Polars DataFrame with standardized structure.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L13973-L14100){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
Graph.get_edge_df()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Edge DataFrame](/assets/graphs/get-edge-df.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The shared four-node graph with node ids 1 to 4 on the nodes and edge ids 1 to 5 on the edges. The result is a table with the columns id, source, target, and rel: one row per edge sorted by id, with endpoints given as node ids and rel empty (null)." width="100%"}

Returns all edges with a consistent column structure:

- column 1: `id` (i64, mandatory, sorted ascending)
- column 2: `source` (i64, mandatory)
- column 3: `target` (i64, mandatory)
- column 4: `rel` (str, mandatory, null if not set)
- columns 5-N: Other attributes (alphabetically sorted)
- weight is included as a special attribute if present

The DataFrame is always sorted by edge ID in ascending order.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [pl.DataFrame]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A Polars DataFrame with edges sorted by `id`. Returns an empty DataFrame if the graph
    has no edges.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The layout mirrors `get_node_df()` in spirit: `id`, `source`, `target`, and `rel`
always come first (in that order), followed by `weight` and any other custom edge
attributes in alphabetical order, with rows sorted by `id` ascending. `weight` is
treated as a first-class attribute column rather than a bare number so it can be
filtered and aggregated with the rest of the schema. Missing `rel` values appear as
`None`.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Every edge DataFrame starts with `id`, `source`, `target`, and `rel`, followed by
`weight` and any other custom attributes:

::: {#69efda17 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = (
    ec.Graph(directed=True)
    .add_nodes(["A", "B", "C", "D"])
    .add_edges(
        [
            ("A", "B", 1.5, {"rel": "friend"}),
            ("B", "C", 2.0, {"rel": "colleague"}),
            ("C", "D", 1.0, {"rel": "friend"}),
        ]
    )
)

df = graph.get_edge_df()
df.columns
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['id', 'source', 'target', 'rel', 'weight']
```
:::
:::


::: {#25bf615d .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
df.shape
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(3, 5)
```
:::
:::


Being a regular Polars DataFrame, it supports the usual filtering and
aggregation -- for example, isolating edges of a given relationship type:

::: {#2bfb5c73 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import polars as pl
df.filter(pl.col("rel") == "friend")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (2, 5)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>rel</th><th>weight</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>str</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>&quot;friend&quot;</td><td>1.5</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>&quot;friend&quot;</td><td>1.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


Or grouping to count how many edges fall into each relationship type:

::: {#f23c7283 .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
df.group_by("rel").len()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (2, 2)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>rel</th><th>len</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>u32</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;colleague&quot;</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;friend&quot;</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


Aggregating `weight` per group works the same way as with any Polars DataFrame:

::: {#95dd3db8 .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
df.group_by("rel").agg(pl.col("weight").mean())
```

